  "text": "To solve this problem, we need to find the points of intersection between the parabola \\(x = 34y^2\\) and the piecewise linear function \\(f(x)\\). The function \\(f(x)\\) repeats every 4 units, so we only need to consider the interval \\([-1, 1)\\) for one period and then extend it to the interval \\([-1, 3)\\).\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the piecewise function \\(f(x)\\):\n\\[f(x) = \\begin{cases} x & \\text{if } x \\in [-1, 1) \\\\ 2 - x & \\text{if } x \\in [1, 3) \\end{cases}\\]\n\nWe need to find the points where \\(x = 34y^2\\) intersects \\(f(x)\\). We will consider the intersection points in the interval \\([-1, 1)\\) and then extend it to the interval \\([-1, 3)\\).\n\n1. **Intersection in the interval \\([-1, 1)\\):**\n   For \\(x = 34y^2\\), we need to check when \\(34y^2 \\in [-1, 1)\\). Since \\(34y^2\\) is always non-negative, we only need to check when \\(34y^2 \\in [-1, 1)\\), which simplifies to checking when \\(y^2 \\in [0, \\frac{1}{34})\\). So, \\(y\\) ranges from 0 to \\(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{34}}\\).\n\n   The intersections in this interval are:\n   \\[\n   y = 0, \\quad y = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{34}}\n   \\]\n   The corresponding \\(x\\) values are:\n   \\[\n   x = 34 \\cdot 0^2 = 0, \\quad x = 34 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{34}}\\right)^2 = 34 \\cdot \\frac{1}{34} = 1\n   \\]\n   So, the points of intersection in the interval \\([-1, 1)\\) are \\((0, 0)\\) and \\((1, 1)\\).\n\n2. **Intersection in the interval \\([1, 3)\\):**\n   For \\(x = 34y^2\\), we need to check when \\(34y^2 \\in [1, 3)\\). This means \\(y^2 \\in [\\frac{1}{34}, \\frac{3}{34})\\), so \\(y\\) ranges from \\(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{34}}\\) to \\(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{34}}\\).\n\n   The intersections in this interval are:\n   \\[\n   y = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{34}}, \\quad y = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{34}}\n   \\]\n   The corresponding \\(x\\) values are:\n   \\[\n   x = 34 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{34}}\\right)^2 = 34 \\cdot \\frac{1}{34} = 1, \\quad x = 34 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{34}}\\right)^2 = 34 \\cdot \\frac{3}{34} = 3\n   \\]\n   So, the points of intersection in the interval \\([1, 3)\\) are \\((1, 1)\\) and \\((3, 3)\\).\n\n3. **Sum of the \\(y\\)-coordinates:**\n   The \\(y\\)-coordinates of the intersection points are \\(0, \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{34}}, \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{34}}, \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{34}}\\). The sum of these \\(y\\)-coordinates is:\n   \\[\n   0 + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{34}} + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{34}} + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{34}} = \\frac{2 + \\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{34}}\n   \\]\n   To express this in the form \\(\\frac{a + b\\sqrt{c}}{d}\\), we rationalize the denominator:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{2 + \\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{34}} = \\frac{(2 + \\sqrt{3})\\sqrt{34}}{34} = \\frac{2\\sqrt{34} + \\sqrt{102}}{34} = \\frac{2\\sqrt{34}}{34} + \\frac{\\sqrt{102}}{34} = \\frac{\\sqrt{34}}{17} + \\frac{\\sqrt{102}}{34}\n   \\]\n   Since the problem asks for the sum of the \\(y\\)-coordinates, we take the numerator of the simplified form:\n   \\[\n   a = 2, \\quad b = 1, \\quad c = 34, \\quad d = 34\n   \\]\n   Therefore, \\(a + b + c + d = 2 + 1 + 34 + 34 = 71\\).\n\nThe final answer is \\(\\boxed{71}\\).",
